WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.690
In English, one small
word does two jobs.

2
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:04.970
"I have a dog.

3
00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:06.230
" "I have eaten.

4
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.870
" Spanish handles the first
one easily: Tengo un perro.

5
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.630
Sam knows the word
for "eaten": comido.

6
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.290
So does the same verb go in front?

7
00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.590
Or does Spanish need a different one?

8
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.810
What goes in front of comido?

9
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.710
In this mission, you'll build
the present perfect from two

10
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.820
parts and meet the six
forms of its helper verb.

11
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.990
You'll make regular past
participles with two endings:

12
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.510
-ado and -ido.

13
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.690
And you'll learn the common
verbs that refuse to follow the

14
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.530
rule.

15
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.110
Your mission: find out what
Spanish puts in front of comido.
